---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import ApiName from '@/components/ApiName.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Resize from '@/components/motion/scenes/Resize.astro';
import { ResizeControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/ResizeControls';
import { MotionLayoutReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-layout.tsx';
import { MotionLayoutAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-layout.ts';
import MotionLayoutSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-layout.svelte';

const defineCardMove = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// A layout motion has no keyframes: the element moves from its old place and size to its new one.
defineMotion('card-move', { verb: 'layout', timing: 'spring.slow.spatial' });`;

// The move after an inline style: a new state calls play(), which compares the layout once the style is applied.
const inlineStyleMove = {
  html: `<div id="card" data-motion="reflow" style="width: 60%">
  Widen me
</div>

<script type="module">
  const card = document.getElementById('card');
  card.style.width = '100%';
  // A new state calls play(): the layout compares.
  card.dataset.motionState = 'wide';
</script>`,
  react: `// A new state calls play() once the width is written.
<Motion
  name="reflow"
  state={wide}
  style={{ width: wide ? '100%' : '60%' }}
>
  Widen me
</Motion>`,
  angular: `<!-- A new state calls play() once the width is set. -->
<div
  udxMotion="reflow"
  [udxMotionState]="wide()"
  [style.width]="wide() ? '100%' : '60%'"
>
  Widen me
</div>`,
  svelte: `<!-- A new state calls play() once the width is set. -->
<div
  style:width={wide ? '100%' : '60%'}
  {@attach motion('reflow', () => ({ state: wide }))}
>
  Widen me
</div>`,
};
---
<MotionPage
  slug="layout"
  title="Motion: move to a new place"
  description="An element with a layout motion moves to its new place and size when the layout around it changes; its content keeps its shape."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Put <code>data-motion="reflow"</code> on an element: when the layout around it changes, it moves from its old place and size to its new one.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion name="reflow"&gt;</code>: when the layout around it changes, it moves from its old place and size to its new one.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion="reflow"</code> on the element: when the layout around it changes, it moves from its old place and size to its new one.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion('reflow')</code> to the element: when the layout around it changes, it moves from its old place and size to its new one.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="resize" label="Live example: Move to a new place" height={[520, 528]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><ResizeControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Resize size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Move an element when its layout changes</h2>
  <p>
    A layout motion has no keyframes. When the layout around its element changes, the element moves from its old place and
    size to its new one, and its content keeps its shape. The preset <code>reflow</code> is a layout motion, and
    <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code> defines it: import that once in your app, as the examples do.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Put <code>data-motion="reflow"</code> on the element, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page.</p>
    <p slot="react">Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion name="reflow"&gt;</code>. It follows the layout of its scope with no call of your own.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, and put <code>udxMotion="reflow"</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion('reflow')</code> to the element. It follows the layout of its scope with no call of your own.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    The examples change an attribute, a class and the order of the rows. Each change is observed, so none of them calls
    <code>play()</code>.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-layout" previewHeight={[416, 432]}>
    <MotionLayoutReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionLayoutAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionLayoutSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Give a layout its own timing</h2>
  <p>To give a layout another timing, define it with <code>defineMotion()</code>. The definition is the same in every framework:</p>
  <Code data-code={defineCardMove} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    Then bind it by its name, as you would the preset:
    <ApiName html={'data-motion="card-move"'} react={'name="card-move"'} angular={'udxMotion="card-move"'} svelte="motion('card-move')" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    The layout engine loads the first time a layout motion is bound. A change made before it has loaded does not move:
    <code>await loadLayoutEngine()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>, loads it in advance.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose the scope of the move</h2>
  <p>
    A change inside the scope moves the elements of the scope: an attribute or a class changes on one of them, a child is
    added, removed or reordered, or <code>hidden</code> changes. The scope is the closest ancestor with
    <code>data-motion-scope</code>, or else the element's parent. Put <code>data-motion-scope</code> on the container of the
    elements that must move together, in any framework: the live example puts it on its container.
  </p>
  <p>An element that appears or disappears does not animate itself: it has no earlier box to move from.</p>

  <h2>Move after an inline style</h2>
  <p>
    The layout engine does not observe an inline style: it watches the attributes, the classes and the children, but not the
    <code>style</code> attribute. Give the element the value that changes as its
    <ApiName html="data-motion-state" react="state" angular="[udxMotionState]" svelte="state" />: each new value calls
    <code>play()</code>, which compares the layout once the new style is applied, and plays the move. An interrupted move
    restarts with the velocity it had.
  </p>
  <Code codes={inlineStyleMove} />
  <p>
    From a control, <code>play('layout')</code> does the same:
    <ApiName html="getMotion(card, 'layout')?.play()" react="motionRef.current?.play('layout')" angular="card.play('layout')" svelte="getMotion(card, 'layout')?.play()" />.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions verb="layout" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>A layout moves on the default spatial spring, unless its definition gives it another timing.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.slow.spatial']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, the new layout applies at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a moving element holds content deeper than three levels, or more than 24 elements, the content beyond that does not follow the move: give each part that must keep its shape its own <code>reflow</code>.</li>
    <li>If you put text directly in a moving element, with no element around it, the text is stretched during the move: wrap it in a <code>span</code>.</li>
    <li>If a change happens outside the scope, the element is not animated: put the change and the element in one scope, with <code>data-motion-scope</code> on their common ancestor.</li>
    <li>If you set <code>transform-origin</code> on a moving element, leave it at the centre: the move scales the element from its centre.</li>
    <li>If the element is an SVG element, binding a layout motion throws: wrap the SVG in an HTML element, since a layout motion needs a box.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
